Z||000000||leroy||04-22-99||09:53 PM||||well i just lost the pass. side headlight motor today, tore the whole thing apart and found a stripped gear inside, wouldnt climb the spur gear to trip the internal switch, anyway i called the guy i bought the car from(he has another 84 2m4 parts car no luck on the headlight motors) and he said the headlight motors from a firebird were the same any truth to this??  the car's an 84 2m4
Z||000001||86_Fastback||04-23-99||06:15 AM||||I have heard that as well, except that the motors are reversed.  So a pass. side Fiero motor is the driver side motor on a Firebird.  I am not positive on this since.  Maybe someone else can back me up (or set me straight as the case might be.)<br>Gregg
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-23-99||07:04 AM||||If you clean out the debris from the stripped out gear you can usually turn the gear around 180 degrees and re-install it.  Through its travel in the headlight assembly the gear turns less than 180 degrees.  I know of several people who have done this.  Note the position of the gear and lever before you take them out so you can get them back in the same relative position<br>While you have the morot open clean and grease the gears.  You might also check the wire connectors at the limit switches and clean the brushes and commutator with some fine sandpaper.  I had a loose connection on one.  I simply soldered it and it has been working fine.  Also use some RTV sealant on the motor housing to seal out moisture.<br>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000003||PaUL||04-23-99||01:53 PM||||Yeah that gear flipping trick worked for me many years ago. 
